Fillets: two roundings that meet across a notch in the rim they round
(mitcad#239).

A rim rounded on both sides of a small notch whose edges are not rounded
(a plate's top edge along an arc of one cylinder, the arc broken at its
lowest point by a V notch 0.4 wide and 0.1 deep; the rounding's radius 0.3,
so that its contact on the top passes above the notch): the edge chain
stops on both sides of the notch, one stripe ends at each side, or both
ends of one stripe meet there. Both blends are the same torus, and over
the notch the ball rolls on with its contact on the top, the notch's faces
cutting the blend's other side; the bodies stored in the files have one
torus face across the notch. OCCT ended each stripe at its vertex of the
notch: SplitKPart ran the contact on the top on to the end of its domain
on that face (for the corner's intersection), here round the whole other
half of the outline, 15.9 long, and the corner (PerformIntersectionAtEnd)
did not cut it back. The two stripes' contacts on the top then overlapped
(ChFi3d_StripeEdgeInter: "fillets have too big radiuses"), or a stripe
whose two ends met there gave self-intersecting wires.

- ChFi3d_JoinAcrossNotches (after the stripes are computed, before the
  corners): two ends, each the only stripe end at its vertex, whose
  analytic blends are one surface, whose contacts on a common face lie on
  one line inside that face over the gap, and whose other contacts end on
  the arcs of two faces of one surface (the notched rim). The contacts on
  the common face are cut back to the ends' sections; the blend of one end
  runs on over the gap, cut by the faces of the notch in turn (the pieces
  of ChFi3d_KPartChainPieces, as over a hole of a face, 0055 and 0071), up
  to the other end's section. The two ends share that section's points and
  are free ends (no corner at their vertices). Where the two blends' tori
  are one surface with other parameters (axes turned or reversed), the
  joined end's pieces take the other's surface, their p-curves mapped by
  the isometry of the parameters, and the blends are one face; at the two
  ends of one stripe, whose blend would be one face closed on itself, and
  where the surfaces are not mapped, the ends share the section's edge.
- ChFi3d_KPartChainPieces takes the ends of the chain as given points
  where the piece before or after is missing (the other stripe's end).
- ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece: a vertex of the face beyond where it meets the
  face of the contact running on and the other contact's face is not taken
  as an edge of the face beyond ending under the blend: its edge with the
  other contact's face lies under the blend between the cut and that
  contact and goes with both faces.

Upstream: new; ends that do not meet these conditions keep OCCT's corners.
